# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# @hltape/api - server environment
#
# Copy this file to apps/api/.env and fill it in:
#   cp apps/api/.env.example apps/api/.env
#
# Every variable below is parsed and validated once, at startup, by
# apps/api/src/lib/config.ts. If anything REQUIRED is missing or malformed the
# server refuses to boot and prints the full list of problems at once.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# REQUIRED - PostgreSQL connection string.
# Any Postgres 14+ instance works: local Docker, Neon, Supabase, RDS.
DATABASE_URL=postgresql://user:password@host:port/database?sslmode=require

# REQUIRED - secret used by Better Auth to sign sessions.
# Generate with: openssl rand -base64 32
BETTER_AUTH_SECRET=

# REQUIRED - public base URL of the auth endpoints exposed by THIS server.
# Locally that is the API origin plus /auth. This is the host that SETS the
# session cookie, so read the cookie section below before splitting it off onto
# a host of its own.
BETTER_AUTH_URL=http://localhost:4000/auth

# REQUIRED - origin of the dashboard (apps/app) allowed to call this API.
# Used for CORS and for SIWE message verification.
APP_ORIGIN=http://localhost:3000

# REQUIRED - 64 hex characters (32 bytes). Encrypts agent wallet private keys
# at rest in the database. Losing or rotating it makes every stored agent
# wallet unreadable, so back it up.
# Generate with: openssl rand -hex 32
MASTER_KEY_HEX=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Where sessions work, and where they do not
#
# The session cookie is set by THIS server, on the host in BETTER_AUTH_URL, and
# the dashboard at APP_ORIGIN has to be able to send it back. By default the
# cookie is host-only - no Domain attribute - so the browser keeps it for
# exactly that one host. Three cases:
#
#   1. One host for both. Nothing to configure. That is the local setup above:
#      APP_ORIGIN=http://localhost:3000 with the API on :4000 is one cookie
#      host, because a port is not part of a cookie's scope. Same for any
#      deployment that serves the dashboard and the API from one hostname
#      behind a proxy - that is the simplest thing to run and it needs nothing
#      set here.
#
#   2. Two subdomains of one domain you own, e.g. the dashboard on
#      app.example.com and this API on api.example.com. API calls themselves
#      still work, but the dashboard reads the session cookie on ITS OWN host
#      to decide whether you are signed in, and a host-only cookie never gets
#      there - so you sign in and land back on /login. Set AUTH_COOKIE_DOMAIN
#      below to example.com.
#
#   3. Two unrelated domains, e.g. the dashboard on a *.vercel.app subdomain
#      and this API on *.fly.dev. Not supported. No Domain value can span two
#      registrable domains, and the SameSite=Lax cookie this server sets is not
#      sent on cross-site requests at all. Put both behind one domain you own
#      (case 1 or 2) before going any further.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# optional - domain to scope the auth cookies to. EMPTY IS THE RIGHT ANSWER for
# case 1 and case 3 above; set it only for case 2.
#   - Write the bare domain: example.com. No scheme, no port, no path; a
#     leading dot is accepted and ignored.
#   - It has to cover the hosts in both BETTER_AUTH_URL and APP_ORIGIN. The
#     server refuses to boot if it does not.
#   - It has to be a domain you own, not a shared suffix. example.co.uk is a
#     domain; co.uk is not, and neither is vercel.app, fly.dev, onrender.com or
#     pages.dev. Browsers silently drop a cookie scoped to a suffix like those,
#     which looks exactly like a login that never sticks. This is not checked
#     here - that would mean shipping the public suffix list - so check it
#     yourself before setting this.
#   - It widens the session beyond one host: every subdomain under it can read
#     the cookie. On a server that custodies agent wallet private keys, do not
#     set it without needing it.
AUTH_COOKIE_DOMAIN=

# optional - port the API listens on. Read by fastify-cli, whose own fallback is
# 3000 - which collides with the dashboard. Keep this set.
PORT=4000

# optional - "development" or "production". Default: development
# Sentry and the Logtail transport only activate in production.
NODE_ENV=development

# optional - "true" runs against Hyperliquid testnet, anything else is mainnet.
# Default: true. Keep it on testnet until you have tested your deployment.
TESTNET=true

# Revenue (builder fee + referral) is NOT configured here.
# It is compiled in, in src/lib/revenue.ts — edit that file to point the builder
# fee and referral code at yourself, or set both to "" to collect nothing.

# optional - BetterStack (Logtail) source token for shipping structured logs.
# Create a Source (Node.js) at https://betterstack.com/ and copy its token.
# Leave empty to log to stdout only.
LOGTAIL_SOURCE_TOKEN=

# optional - ingest host for the Logtail source above.
# Shown next to the token in the BetterStack source settings.
LOGTAIL_ENDPOINT=

# optional - Sentry DSN for server-side error reporting.
# Leave empty to disable Sentry.
SENTRY_DSN=

# optional - comma-separated list of workers to enable debug logs for.
# Options: brain, feeder, dispatcher, executor, janitor, api, * (all)
DEBUG_WORKERS=

# optional - set to "true" to log worker metrics to the console.
DEBUG_METRICS=false
